description:
Canonical: crate-jmap-mail-server/src/memory.rs:292-296

    #[derive(Debug)]
    pub struct MemoryError(
        /// Human-readable description of the underlying failure.
        pub String,
    );

Sibling drift (5 of 6 add Clone, 1 matches):

  crate-jmap-calendars-server/src/memory.rs:73-74   #[derive(Debug, Clone)] pub struct MemoryError(pub String);
  crate-jmap-chat-server/src/memory.rs:171-172      #[derive(Debug, Clone)] pub struct MemoryError(pub String);
  crate-jmap-contacts-server/src/memory.rs:70-71    #[derive(Debug, Clone)] pub struct MemoryError(pub String);
  crate-jmap-sharing-server/src/memory.rs:176-177   #[derive(Debug, Clone)] pub struct MemoryError(pub String);
  crate-jmap-tasks-server/src/memory.rs:76-77       #[derive(Debug, Clone)] pub struct MemoryError(pub String);
  crate-jmap-filenode-server/src/memory.rs:68-69    #[derive(Debug)] (matches canonical)

Why this matters even though Clone is cheap: per workspace AGENTS.md 'Canonical Templates', extension-server siblings cookie-cut from jmap-mail-server. Any divergence is a regression of the cookie-cutter intent and forces a future maintainer to wonder which one is right when the next backend is added. Error types in Rust idiomatically do NOT derive Clone unless callers explicitly need to copy them — std::io::Error doesn't, anyhow::Error doesn't. The canonical got the idiom right; the siblings drifted.

Why P3 not P2: Clone on a String-wrapping error is genuinely cheap and harmless at the call site; only the consistency angle bites.
